Abstract
Delaying flexible batch jobs on high-performance computing (HPC) systems to hours of lower grid carbon
intensity can cut operational emissions, but a deployed scheduler sees only a past-only forecast and the
walltime that users request. This pre-registered measurement replays three real HPC job traces as a coun-
terfactual on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year and proposes no new scheduler. At 12 h
of slack, delay scheduling with a past-only LightGBM forecast and the requested walltime saved 6.6, 13.2
and 5.3 g CO2 per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2 (a post-hoc conversion of the
pre-registered relative saving), or 1.9 %, 3.8 % and 1.5 % of run-now emissions. The same planner given
perfect information saved more, and the deployable policy retained 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. The
pre-registered test is confirmatory on PM100 and a replication on the other two traces. Replacing the re-
quested walltime by a prediction from the user's recent jobs raised the saving on all three traces. A Shapley
split of the remaining gap into forecast and runtime terms is descriptive and trace-specific. Night-only
and persistence rules saved little or increased emissions. The savings are bounds under free capacity, use
production-average intensity rather than avoided marginal emissions, and carry intervals conditional on the
sampled workload. The replay suggests that runtime information may be worth evaluating in production
schedulers.
Keywords: carbon-aware scheduling, HPC batch jobs, temporal shifting, walltime estimates, carbon
intensity forecasting, pre-registration, Shapley decomposition

1. Introduction

    Computing draws a notable share of electricity. Data-center energy use grew far more slowly than
workloads up to 2018 [1], but high-performance computing (HPC) systems, which serve queues of batch jobs
on thousands of nodes, add a carbon footprint that recent estimates describe as significant [2]. Operational
emissions per kilowatt-hour are not constant. The carbon intensity of grid electricity changes by the hour
with the generation mix, so the same job emits different amounts of CO2 depending on when and where

    Corresponding author: [CORRESPONDING_AUTHOR_EMAIL]
it runs [3]. A batch scheduler can use this variation without new hardware, because many jobs tolerate a
late start and the scheduler may hold a job, within a window of slack, until a lower-carbon hour. Doing so
requires two inputs that a deployed scheduler must supply for itself: a forecast of carbon intensity and an
estimate of how long each job will run.

    Temporal shifting of this kind is well studied. Simulation studies place delay-tolerant jobs at low-carbon
hours [4�8], a production system shifts flexible load with day-ahead forecasts [9], and the forecast itself has
been developed as a separate problem [10, 11]. These studies mostly give the scheduler the true carbon
signal or a noise-perturbed copy of it, and most give it the true job length. Wiesner et al. compare an
optimal signal with forecasts that carry injected error, Sukprasert et al. include a prediction-error scenario,
and Attenni et al. state that runtimes are assumed available to the scheduler [4�6]. The distance between a
forecast-driven and a perfect-information scheduler is therefore already known; ReBase reports it by forecast
horizon for wind-powered data centers [12].

    A deployed HPC scheduler faces a different input set. Its forecast is built from past data only, and its
runtime information is the walltime that the user requested at submission, an upper bound that the system
enforces by killing the job. Users tend to request far more than they need. Average runtime-to-estimate
accuracy was between 31 and 47 percent on four production logs [13], and on the three traces used here
the median analyzed job ran for 14.3 %, 30.6 % and 17.8 % of its requested time (PM100, F-DATA and KIT
FH2).

    Studies that vary forecast error inject synthetic noise or assume ideal job lengths, and studies on real
job traces assume known runtimes [4�6, 11, 14, 15]. The saving a deployable scheduler realizes with a
trained past-only forecast and the user-requested walltime, and how the lost saving divides between those
two inputs, is not reported in the studies we reviewed. A saving that holds only under perfect inputs is an
upper bound, and a decision to adopt carbon-aware delay needs the saving that survives deployment.

    We measure that saving in a pre-registered replay of three real HPC job traces on 42 U.S. balancing
authorities over a held-out year. At 12 h of slack, delay scheduling with a past-only LightGBM forecast
and the requested walltime saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO2 per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and
KIT FH2 (a post-hoc conversion of the pre-registered relative saving). Run-now means were 350, 347 and
349 g CO2 per kWh, so the relative savings are 1.9 %, 3.8 % and 1.5 % of run-now emissions. The same
planner given perfect information saved more, and the deployable policy kept 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that
saving. Replacing the requested walltime by a prediction from the user's earlier jobs raised the saving on
all three traces. The pre-registered test of the deployable saving is confirmatory on PM100 and a secondary
replication on the other two traces. Four limits frame these numbers. The replay is a counterfactual:
jobs from non-U.S. systems run on U.S. signals at their local clock time. The signal is production-average
intensity, so the savings are not avoided marginal emissions. The oracle is the same heuristic with perfect
inputs, not a global optimum. The simulation has no queue, so the results are bounds under free capacity.

