Delaying flexible batch jobs on high-performance computing (HPC) systems to hours of lower grid carbon intensity can cut operational emissions, but a deployed scheduler sees only a past-only forecast and the walltime that users request. This pre-registered measurement replays three real HPC job traces as a counterfactual on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year and proposes no new scheduler. At 12 h of slack, delay scheduling with a past-only LightGBM forecast and the requested walltime saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO$_2$ per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2 (a post-hoc conversion of the pre-registered relative saving), or 1.9\,\%, 3.8\,\% and 1.5\,\% of run-now emissions. The same planner given perfect information saved more, and the deployable policy retained 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. The pre-registered test is confirmatory on PM100 and a replication on the other two traces. Replacing the requested walltime by a prediction from the user's recent jobs raised the saving on all three traces. A Shapley split of the remaining gap into forecast and runtime terms is descriptive and trace-specific. Night-only and persistence rules saved little or increased emissions. The savings are bounds under free capacity, use production-average intensity rather than avoided marginal emissions, and carry intervals conditional on the sampled workload. The replay suggests that runtime information may be worth evaluating in production schedulers.
\end{abstract}

