\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Computing draws a notable share of electricity. Data-center energy use grew far more slowly than workloads up to 2018 \cite{masanet2020}, but high-performance computing (HPC) systems, which serve queues of batch jobs on thousands of nodes, add a carbon footprint that recent estimates describe as significant \cite{li2023sc}. Operational emissions per kilowatt-hour are not constant. The carbon intensity of grid electricity changes by the hour with the generation mix, so the same job emits different amounts of CO$_2$ depending on when and where it runs \cite{dodge2022}. A batch scheduler can use this variation without new hardware, because many jobs tolerate a late start and the scheduler may hold a job, within a window of slack, until a lower-carbon hour. Doing so requires two inputs that a deployed scheduler must supply for itself: a forecast of carbon intensity and an estimate of how long each job will run.

Temporal shifting of this kind is well studied. Simulation studies place delay-tolerant jobs at low-carbon hours \cite{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits,suscom_101498,suscom_101407,cucumber2022}, a production system shifts flexible load with day-ahead forecasts \cite{radovanovic2023}, and the forecast itself has been developed as a separate problem \cite{maji2022carboncast,joseph2025}. These studies mostly give the scheduler the true carbon signal or a noise-perturbed copy of it, and most give it the true job length. Wiesner et al.\ compare an optimal signal with forecasts that carry injected error, Sukprasert et al.\ include a prediction-error scenario, and Attenni et al.\ state that runtimes are assumed available to the scheduler \cite{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits,suscom_101498}. The distance between a forecast-driven and a perfect-information scheduler is therefore already known; ReBase reports it by forecast horizon for wind-powered data centers \cite{suscom_101470}.

A deployed HPC scheduler faces a different input set. Its forecast is built from past data only, and its runtime information is the walltime that the user requested at submission, an upper bound that the system enforces by killing the job. Users tend to request far more than they need. Average runtime-to-estimate accuracy was between 31 and 47 percent on four production logs \cite{tsafrir2007}, and on the three traces used here the median analyzed job ran for 14.3\,\%, 30.6\,\% and 17.8\,\% of its requested time (PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2).

Studies that vary forecast error inject synthetic noise or assume ideal job lengths, and studies on real job traces assume known runtimes \cite{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits,suscom_101498,bostandoost2024,feng2026,joseph2025}. The saving a deployable scheduler realizes with a trained past-only forecast and the user-requested walltime, and how the lost saving divides between those two inputs, is not reported in the studies we reviewed. A saving that holds only under perfect inputs is an upper bound, and a decision to adopt carbon-aware delay needs the saving that survives deployment.

We measure that saving in a pre-registered replay of three real HPC job traces on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year. At 12 h of slack, delay scheduling with a past-only LightGBM forecast and the requested walltime saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO$_2$ per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2 (a post-hoc conversion of the pre-registered relative saving). Run-now means were 350, 347 and 349 g CO$_2$ per kWh, so the relative savings are 1.9\,\%, 3.8\,\% and 1.5\,\% of run-now emissions. The same planner given perfect information saved more, and the deployable policy kept 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. Replacing the requested walltime by a prediction from the user's earlier jobs raised the saving on all three traces. The pre-registered test of the deployable saving is confirmatory on PM100 and a secondary replication on the other two traces. Four limits frame these numbers. The replay is a counterfactual: jobs from non-U.S. systems run on U.S. signals at their local clock time. The signal is production-average intensity, so the savings are not avoided marginal emissions. The oracle is the same heuristic with perfect inputs, not a global optimum. The simulation has no queue, so the results are bounds under free capacity. No new scheduler is proposed. Figure~\ref{fig:design} shows what the deployed and the perfect-information scheduler each see.

We use the following terms throughout. Run-now is the carbon-agnostic baseline that starts every job at arrival. The slack $S$ is the maximum delay of a job's start. The requested walltime is the limit that the user supplies at submission and that the scheduler enforces by killing the job (state TIMEOUT); the actual runtime is the measured run time. The planner starts each job at the time within its slack that minimizes the integral of carbon intensity over the job's estimated duration. The perfect-information arm, called the oracle, is the same planner given the realized intensity and, in one cell of the design, the actual runtime. A scheduler is deployable if it uses only what is known at submission, under the data-availability assumption of Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}. Carbon intensity is the average, production-based, location-based intensity of a balancing authority (BA), an entity that balances supply and demand on a section of the U.S. grid and reports to the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA). Marginal intensity, the quantity relevant to a change in demand, is not modeled; the savings are savings in accounted average intensity, and the choice of signal can change them \cite{sukprasert2024avgmarg}.

This paper makes four contributions.
\begin{itemize}
  \item A pre-registered measurement, on real HPC batch-job traces and 42 U.S. BAs with a held-out year, of the share of the perfect-information carbon saving that delay scheduling realizes with a past-only forecast and the requested walltime, reported in absolute and relative terms.
  \item A $2\times 2$ design (signal by runtime estimate) with a descriptive Shapley allocation that divides the gap between the perfect-information and the deployable scheduler into a forecast term and a runtime term, together with the saving recovered by a history-based runtime prediction.
  \item Calendar and synchronized-week intervals for every estimate, an exploratory check of the added uncertainty from the workload sample, and a labeled block of robustness checks, with small and negative results reported as found.
  \item The archiving of code, derived data, the result ledger, the pre-registration and its checksum on Zenodo, with the source repository private until publication.
\end{itemize}

Section~\ref{sec:related} places the study among earlier work. Section~\ref{sec:data} describes the carbon-intensity data and the job traces, and Section~\ref{sec:method} defines the arms, the estimands, the forecaster and the inference. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports the results, Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses them together with the limitations and threats to validity, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.
