\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
% 2.1 Temporal shifting and carbon-aware computing (300 words)
\subsection{Temporal shifting and carbon-aware computing}\label{sec:rel_shifting}

Wiesner et al.\ \cite{wiesner2021} delay nightly and machine-learning jobs against the carbon intensity of four regions in 2020, compare an optimal signal with forecasts that carry injected error, and take the delay tolerance as given. Sukprasert et al.\ \cite{sukprasert2024limits} extend the evaluation to 123 regions with synthetic job classes that differ in length, deadline and interruptibility, report ideal upper bounds beside simple and sophisticated policies, and include a prediction-error scenario. Because that study compares policies of different sophistication, the present study keeps a night rule and a persistence rule beside the planner. Radovanovi\'c et al.\ \cite{radovanovic2023} report a production system that shifts flexible load of a large cloud fleet using day-ahead carbon forecasts, measured in deployment and aimed at cloud load rather than HPC batch jobs.

CarbonCast \cite{maji2022carboncast} forecasts grid carbon intensity several days ahead from source-level forecasts; it is a forecasting method, not a savings study, and the present study instead trains a simpler forecaster on past data only. Further work widens the decision space or the objective. Hanafy et al.\ price the cost of reducing carbon \cite{hanafy2024green}, CarbonScaler scales elastic workloads \cite{hanafy2023carbonscaler}, and CASPER places services across sites and time \cite{souza2023casper}. Cucumber admits delay-tolerant jobs under renewable supply \cite{cucumber2022}, Lechowicz et al.\ schedule dependent batch jobs in data-processing clusters \cite{lechowicz2025}, Hall et al.\ treat forecast uncertainty in data center control \cite{hall2025}, and Lin and Chien modulate data center capacity \cite{linchien2023}. Asadov et al.\ review spatio-temporal shifting \cite{asadov2025}. The jobs studied here are rigid and non-interruptible, so only the start time is a decision.

\subsection{Closest papers in this journal}\label{sec:rel_suscom}

Attenni et al.\ \cite{suscom_101498} simulate spatial, temporal and spatio-temporal shifting of cloud workloads over carbon, water and land use, with Azure Functions and Spark traces whose arrival patterns are synthetic and with a noise-perturbed grid mix as the forecast. They state that job runtimes are assumed known to the scheduler and that a trained operational forecaster remains future work. These are the two inputs measured here. Zhai et al.\ \cite{suscom_101216} schedule delay-tolerant aggregate workload over three wind-powered data centers, forecast workload and wind power with decomposition-LSTM models, and vary injected wind-forecast error; the workload has no per-job runtimes and the signal is wind generation, not grid carbon intensity.

The ReBase study \cite{suscom_101470} compares a forecast-driven mixed-integer baseline with learned correction against an oracle that uses realized wind and workload, and reports the gap by forecast horizon (wind-utilization gap from 1.03 to 11.33 percent). Its tasks are unit tasks of equal energy, so no job runtime enters, and the gap is attributed to forecast error alone. The forecast-to-oracle gap is therefore a known quantity; the present study adds a trained past-only forecaster on grid carbon intensity, real jobs and a second information factor. Liow et al.\ \cite{suscom_101407} select a region and start time from marginal-emissions forecasts for emulated tasks of three lengths under a 12 h latency bound; their interval is over three task profiles, and forecast error is named as a limitation. Chen et al.\ \cite{suscom_100950} schedule the offline load of a cloud trace across Chinese data centers with static regional emission factors and without forecasts or runtime estimates.

Reviews and neighbors appear in one clause each: a systematic review of green-aware management \cite{suscom_100989}, carbon-aware federated orchestration in edge-cloud systems \cite{suscom_101398}, carbon-conscious management of data centers with photovoltaic and battery storage \cite{suscom_101429}, and power and energy models for HPC applications \cite{suscom_101106}.

% 2.3 Runtime estimates, prediction in HPC and the closest carbon-aware works (200 words)
\subsection{Runtime estimates and the closest carbon-aware works}\label{sec:rel_runtime}

A requested walltime is an upper bound that users set above the expected runtime. Mu'alem and Feitelson \cite{mualem2001} find user estimates on three IBM SP2 logs to be rather poor but a good upper bound, because few jobs are killed at their limit. They note that inaccurate estimates need not harm a backfilling scheduler, so the requested-walltime arm here is a loss of planning information, not a defect of the scheduler. Tsafrir et al.\ \cite{tsafrir2007} keep user estimates only as kill times and schedule with the mean runtime of the user's last two jobs, raising average accuracy from between 31 and 47 percent to about 60 to 62 percent on four logs. Machine-learning predictors \cite{gaussier2015}, the penalty of underestimates that kill jobs \cite{fan2017}, and a trace with refined runtime estimates \cite{klusacek2024} extend this line; a status report on parallel job scheduling \cite{feitelson2004} gives the background. These works target waiting time and utilization, not carbon.

Three works outside this journal bound the runtime and real-trace claims. LACS \cite{bostandoost2024} scales elastic jobs of unknown length with a learning-augmented algorithm, using one grid, synthetic job lengths, simulated forecast error and simulated length-prediction error. COAST \cite{feng2026}, an arXiv preprint, recommends start times for HPC jobs on a congestion game, using a public HPC log and one grid with actual runtimes and no forecast. LiteCast \cite{joseph2025}, also a preprint, builds a lightweight forecaster for 50 regions using weather and demand inputs, and replays the KIT FH2 log with job lengths known; its heuristic saves about 2 percent below the oracle there.

\subsection{Position}\label{sec:rel_position}

Table~\ref{tab:related} sets these studies side by side. The forecast-to-oracle gap is known, and the planner here is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of Wiesner et al.\ and Sukprasert et al.; no new scheduler is proposed. None of the five papers from this journal uses a user-requested walltime or splits the gap between two inputs. In our reading, none of the three outside works above reports the saving of a past-only forecast with requested walltimes on real HPC traces. The present study contributes that measurement.

