\section{Data}\label{sec:data}

This section defines the grid signal, the three job traces, the study periods and the counterfactual pairing of jobs with grids, so that a reader can check what each number measures. Table~\ref{tab:data} lists the traces, the periods and the audit counts.

\subsection{Grid carbon intensity}\label{sec:data:grid}

The grid signal comes from the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) Form EIA-930 Hourly Electric Grid Monitor, which reports hourly net generation by fuel for balancing authorities (BAs) \citep{eia930_about}. We use the six-month balance files from 2018 H2 to 2026 H1 for 70 candidate BAs. The data are in the public domain. For BA $b$ and hour $t$, the carbon intensity is the generation-weighted mean of lifecycle emission factors, $c_b(t)=\sum_f g_{b,f}(t)\,\epsilon_f\big/\sum_f g_{b,f}(t)$, in g CO$_2$-equivalent per kWh, where $g_{b,f}$ is generation from fuel $f$ and $\epsilon_f$ its factor. This is a production-based average intensity, the quantity behind location-based Scope 2 accounting. It is not the marginal intensity that determines avoided emissions, which can rank hours differently \citep{sukprasert2024avgmarg,koebrich2025,siler2012}. Imports and storage are not modeled. Production-based construction from the fuel mix follows earlier work \citep{tranberg2019,leerbeck2020,miller2020}.

The factors $\epsilon_f$ are the lifecycle medians of the IPCC fifth assessment report \citep{schlomer2014} for coal, natural gas, nuclear, hydropower, solar and wind, and judgment values for oil, other and unknown fuels, which have no matching row in that table. Thus 6 of the 9 factors are verified against a primary table (the values are in the supplement). The unknown-fuel share is zero in every eligible BA, and an accounting-factor sensitivity (S3) is described in Section~\ref{sec:method:registry}. For ERCO, the only BA compared, the series correlates with EIA's own published CO$_2$ intensity at an hourly $r$ of 0.988, with a mean bias of $-$2.0 g/kWh.

Cleaning follows four fixed rules, none tuned on development or test data. R1 builds a complete hourly UTC grid per BA. R2 flags invalid hours, where generation is missing or not positive. R3 flags outlier hours, where the hour-to-hour change has a robust $z$ score above 8 over a centered 30-day window or intensity leaves the range 0 to 1,100 g/kWh. R4 repairs gaps by linear interpolation for gaps up to 3 h and by the same hour one week earlier for gaps of 4 to 168 h, and it marks the whole UTC day unusable for longer gaps. The counts are in Table~\ref{tab:data}. In the test year, 0.04\,\%, 0.78\,\% and 0.15\,\% of hours are interpolated, outlier-replaced and week-filled. The fuel-column mapping showed no schema-induced jump at the July 2024 schema change in any eligible BA.

A BA is eligible if it reports from the start of the span to its end and has at least 90\,\% valid hours in each of the three periods of Section~\ref{sec:data:periods}. It must also average at least 100 MW and have a training-period standard deviation of intensity above 1 g/kWh. This leaves 42 of the 70 BAs. The 90\,\% threshold replaced a 95\,\% draft before any outcome was scored. The BAs are selected on data coverage and are not a sample, so the inference target is this fixed set (Section~\ref{sec:method:inference}).

The planner needs data that a deployed scheduler would hold at arrival time $a$. The frozen design assumes that the intensity of the hour ending at $\lfloor a\rfloor$ is available at $a$. EIA states that net generation by source is typically published by 11:00 Eastern on the next day, and that values are revised afterwards \citep{eia930_about}. The assumption is therefore optimistic by about a day, and the series we use holds archived, revised values. First-release values are not archived in our data. Two post-freeze sensitivities (S2 and S2b) delay the forecast information by 1 to 6 h and by 24 to 48 h.

\subsection{Job traces}\label{sec:data:jobs}

We replay three public traces of production supercomputers (Table~\ref{tab:data}). PM100 comes from the Marconi100 system at CINECA in Bologna and holds Slurm records with sampled node power \citep{antici2023pm100}. It is the primary trace and carries the confirmatory tests. F-DATA comes from the Fugaku system and holds job records with measured energy \citep{antici2024fdata}; we use five months (April 2021 and January, April, July and October 2023). The third trace, KIT FH2, is the ForHLR II log of the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology in the Parallel Workloads Archive \citep{feitelson2014pwa}. PM100 and F-DATA carry the CC-BY-4.0 license. The archive states no license for KIT FH2 and asks users to acknowledge the provider, which we do; we do not redistribute the raw log. F-DATA and KIT FH2 are secondary replications.

Each trace is standardized to the same fields: submit time in local site time, requested walltime $R_{\mathrm{req}}$, actual runtime $R_{\mathrm{act}}$, final state, user and mean power $P$. Power is a whole-job mean, taken as constant over the run: the mean of the sampled job power series for PM100 and the average power field for F-DATA, never multiplied by the node count. Median power in PM100 scales linearly with allocated nodes, which confirms that the figure is already a whole-job value. KIT FH2 has no power data, so each job is given 1 kW and its energy weight is proportional to runtime. The main filter keeps jobs in state COMPLETED with $R_{\mathrm{act}}\ge 1$ min and $0<R_{\mathrm{req}}\le 72$ h. After the filter the traces hold 394, 1{,}257 and 165 users.

Requested walltime is a limit that users set with a margin, and its semantics differ by trace. The median ratio of actual to requested runtime among the jobs that pass the main filter is 0.143 in PM100, 0.306 in F-DATA and 0.178 in KIT FH2 (Figure~\ref{fig:walltime}). Older studies of other machines also find that user estimates are poor \citep{tsafrir2007} but usually safe upper bounds \citep{mualem2001}. PM100 holds 8{,}689 jobs in state TIMEOUT, and 92.2\,\% of them ended within 2\,\% of the limit. F-DATA records no timeouts. Every KIT FH2 job has status completed, so timeouts cannot be identified there, although 7.3\,\% of its jobs run to within 2\,\% of the limit. Jobs that run to the limit are censored in runtime; we return to them in Section~\ref{sec:method:registry} and in the limitations. In the raw PM100 log, jobs longer than 24 h supply 14.1\,\% of the energy, and no job that passes the main filter is that long.

The standardization dropped no rows. A job table records completed work and not a queue, so it carries no capacity constraint, and the replay is a bound under free capacity. A fourth trace, the NLR Kestrel jobs data, would have matched both the EIA-930 years and a U.S. site, but its host returned a maintenance error on two attempts and its license could not be read, so it is not used. As a consequence, no trace in the study comes from a U.S. site or from the grid years that are replayed.

\subsection{Periods and the counterfactual pairing}\label{sec:data:periods}

Three consecutive periods separate design from scoring. TRAIN (2018-07-01 to 2024-06-30) fits the forecasters and the historical profile. DEV (2024-07-01 to 2025-06-30) supports every design choice. TEST (2025-07-01 to 2026-06-30) was scored once, after the pre-registration was frozen. Because of whole-week placement and a 100 h margin at the end of the period, test arrivals fall between 2025-07-07 and 2026-06-14.

The traces were recorded in 2016 to 2018 (KIT FH2), 2020 (PM100) and 2021 and 2023 (F-DATA), and the grids are those of 2025 to 2026 in the United States. Pairing them is a what-if. A job that arrived on a given weekday and clock time at its home site is replayed on a U.S. grid at that weekday and clock time in the BA's standard local time. The result describes how this kind of workload would fare on these grids, and not what any site achieved. A post-freeze check shifts the clock by 3 h in either direction to test the dependence on placement (Section~\ref{sec:method:registry}). Figure~\ref{fig:grids} shows how much the BAs differ.

