\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{What the $2\times 2$ describes}\label{sec:disc:twobytwo}

The design separates two inputs that a deployed scheduler lacks: the realized carbon signal and the actual runtime. Giving the planner perfect intensity but only the requested walltime (oracle|req) saves 3.33\,\%, 5.42\,\% and 2.37\,\% of run-now emissions on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2, against 5.75\,\%, 7.22\,\% and 4.02\,\% with the actual runtime as well. This walltime-only cost parallels the forecast-only gap that earlier work reports for an oracle against a forecast-driven scheduler \cite{suscom_101470}.

The Shapley allocation (Eq.~\eqref{eq:shapley}) assigns the remaining gap of D to a forecast term and a runtime term. The forecast term is 1.56\,pp, 1.76\,pp and 1.03\,pp, and the runtime term is 2.30\,pp, 1.65\,pp and 1.48\,pp. The difference between them has a calendar interval and a synchronized-week interval that both exclude zero on PM100 and KIT FH2, where the runtime term is larger, and both include zero on F-DATA, where the terms are comparable. An ordering is therefore trace-specific, and we state none for the three traces together.

The runtime term is consistent with loose requests: the median analyzed job ran for 14.3\,\%, 30.6\,\% and 17.8\,\% of its requested walltime, so a planner that trusts the request reserves a window far longer than the job needs. The interaction is not zero (0.24\,pp, 0.29\,pp and 0.34\,pp), so the two losses do not add. The allocation is descriptive; it does not measure the causal effect of improving either input.

\subsection{Implications as hypotheses}\label{sec:disc:implications}

The replay suggests several hypotheses for future evaluation. For operators, logging the requested and the actual runtime and adding a history-based predictor to a delay planner could be worth testing in a production scheduler, because the prediction raised the saving by 1.03\,pp, 0.53\,pp and 0.35\,pp in the replay. The predictor is deliberately simple, so these values may understate what job history can recover.

For users, accurate walltime requests may carry a carbon value, since the requested walltime cost 2.18\,pp, 1.50\,pp and 1.31\,pp relative to the actual runtime. A limit set below the true runtime kills the job, so the implication concerns better estimates, not shorter limits.

For scheduler designers, the forecast and the runtime estimate act as separate levers, and the learned forecaster beat the seven-day profile on all three traces by 0.79\,pp, 0.47\,pp and 0.53\,pp. The planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of earlier work, so nothing here ranks schedulers.

For providers of carbon signals, data latency may matter. When the forecast information is delayed by 6 h, the deployable saving on PM100 is \pendingkey{} of run-now emissions, to be read beside the frozen 1.90\,\%.

The absolute effect is modest. D saves 47, 126 and 27 g CO$_2$ per job as a mean over jobs in the pooled replay, and the replay says nothing about the saving a specific site would see.

\subsection{Comparison with earlier work}\label{sec:disc:compare}

A slack of 12 h on a single grid may give smaller savings than studies that move work across regions or follow wind output, because a delay can only move a job within the variation of one grid's average intensity. We draw no numeric comparison with their headline figures. Attenni et al.\ assume known runtimes and a noise-perturbed grid mix \cite{suscom_101498}, and their remark that runtimes could be estimated from previous runs is the position that the prediction arm tests here. LiteCast replays the same KIT FH2 log with a lightweight forecaster, but with actual job lengths and the recorded wait as slack \cite{joseph2025}, so its savings and ours are not comparable. The released code and result ledger let a reader repeat any cell of these comparisons.

\subsection{Limitations and threats to validity}\label{sec:disc:threats}

Each paragraph names one threat, says why the result may still hold, and points to the analysis that addresses it. Analyses run after the freeze are labeled post-hoc or exploratory throughout.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{A. Counterfactual replay on foreign traces.} Marconi100 (2020), Fugaku and KIT ForHLR II (2016 to 2018) are placed on U.S. production signals of the test year, and a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel could not be obtained. The agreement of three foreign traces does not establish U.S. behavior, and a balancing authority's standard time set against trace daylight saving can shift summer arrivals by an hour. The study is therefore a what-if replay, not a deployment forecast. A placement-shift check (exploratory, PM100) moves arrivals by 3 h in either direction; the saving of D changes by \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} percentage points for the earlier and the later shift (Section~\ref{sec:results}).

\medskip\noindent\textbf{B. Average, not marginal, intensity.} The signal is production-average intensity from EIA-930; imports and storage are excluded and three of the nine emission factors are unverified. A saving in accounted intensity need not equal avoided marginal emissions, and the ordering of arms may change under a marginal signal. The claim is limited to modeled operational emissions on average intensity. S3 refits the factor sets (\pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} for D on PM100), and X7 uses a fossil-only proxy.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{C. The oracle is not a global optimum.} The oracle is the same heuristic with perfect inputs, so the retained share is relative to it and has no slack-free meaning. We report the absolute saving and the slack curve before the share, and state the share per trace with its interval (Section~\ref{sec:method:inference}).

\medskip\noindent\textbf{D. No queue or capacity.} The replay has no queue, backfilling, idle power or interruptibility, and delayed jobs may concentrate load. The savings are bounds under free capacity, and interruptible jobs are not covered. X4 is a congestion indicator only: the hourly peak-to-mean ratio of D relative to run-now is \pendingkey{} on PM100, and it cannot establish feasibility.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{E. Interval scope.} The calendar interval is conditional on the observed job sample and treats BAs as independent; PM100 is one 2020 trace of 394 users after filtering. The synchronized-week interval (PH1) changes the width of the D interval by factors of 1.71, 0.93 and 2.28, while both intervals condition on the sampled jobs. The exploratory workload-sample check (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty and gives \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{} for D on PM100.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{F. Carbon-weighted pooling.} The pooled estimand weights BAs by carbon, so high-carbon grids dominate, and every BA reuses the same job pool. The per-BA distribution of the runtime penalty (PH3) reports the spread beside the pooled value.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{G. Data vintage.} The planner assumes that the intensity of the hour ending at arrival is available at arrival, and the archived series holds revised values. EIA publishes generation by source about a day later and revises it afterwards \cite{eia930_about}. S2 delays the forecast information by 1 to 6 h and isolates delay, not revision, which remains unmeasured.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{H. Constant per-job power.} Power is a whole-job mean taken as constant, KIT FH2 carries 1 kW per job, and energy weighting favors long jobs (in the raw PM100 log, jobs over 24 h supply 14.1\,\% of the energy, but no analyzed PM100 job is that long). The unweighted outcome (the u rows of the ledger) and the job-length classes address it.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{I. Calendar alignment.} Each job lands in a random whole week of the test year, and the first and last days of the year (about 16 days in all) receive no arrivals. Three replicates and the S1 control show the spread.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{J. Weak runtime baselines.} The prediction (mean of the user's last two jobs) is a deliberately simple predictor, so H3 may understate the recovery of a better one, and the requested walltime is a loose bound by construction. X1 sweeps estimate accuracy, and the padded and quantile arms probe operator rules, both exploratory.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{K. Forecaster selection.} D was chosen on development MAE (it beat the seasonal naive day on 1 to 24 h error in 38 of 42 BAs), and MAE rank is not saving rank. Hyperparameters were frozen before the test year; S5 tunes the forecaster (exploratory), and PH6 compares D with the seven-day profile.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{L. Multiplicity.} Three confirmatory tests (H1 to H3, PM100, Holm) sit beside a large sensitivity grid. P values are given for H1 to H3 only, and the registry (Section~\ref{sec:method:registry}) and the all-cells table list every analysis.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{M. Selection and status limits.} The BA eligibility rule uses test-year availability, and KIT FH2 status codes cannot identify timeouts. S4 (secondary, run after the freeze; PREREG item 6, deviation row 2) includes timeouts on PM100 and gives 2.04\,\% for D, and a near-limit exclusion is reported.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{N. One heuristic.} The planner is a delay-only, non-interruptible policy with no spatial shifting. No scheduler is ranked, and the stated scope is the limit.

\subsection{Reproducibility}\label{sec:disc:repro}

A single script rebuilds every ledger from the stored result files and checks equality. The frozen scripts, the pre-registration and its checksum, the environment lock file, the checksum of every result file and the exact run commands are archived at [ZENODO\_DOI\_PLACEHOLDER], and the code is kept in a private repository ([GITHUB\_LINK\_PLACEHOLDER], to be supplied by the owner). Section~\ref{sec:method:registry} lists the post-freeze analyses with their status.
% 07_conclusion.tmpl.tex: SPEC s5 "7 Conclusion" (250 words). Written by A28. Results first, absolute before relative, no p-values, no new numbers.
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

This study replayed three real HPC job traces on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year to measure how much carbon saving a deployable delay scheduler keeps. At 12 h of slack, a past-only LightGBM forecast with the requested walltime gave a positive modeled saving on all three replays. It saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO$_2$ per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2, or 1.9\,\%, 3.8\,\% and 1.5\,\% of run-now emissions. The same planner with perfect information saved more, and the deployable policy kept 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. A history-based runtime prediction raised the saving on all three traces. The Shapley split of the remaining gap into a forecast term and a runtime term is descriptive: the runtime term was larger on PM100 and KIT FH2, and the terms were comparable on F-DATA.

The replay suggests that logging runtimes and adding a runtime predictor may be worth evaluating in production schedulers. The savings are bounds under free capacity, measured on production-average intensity rather than avoided marginal emissions, on foreign traces placed on U.S. grids, with intervals conditional on the sampled workload. No new scheduler is proposed.

Future work includes marginal-intensity signals, queue-aware simulation, a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel, and tuned forecasters if the exploratory tuning check does not close the question. Code, derived data and result ledgers are archived on Zenodo ([ZENODO DOI: owner to publish]); the source repository is private until publication ([GITHUB LINK: owner to provide]).
