paper/sections/06_discussion.tex:21:For providers of carbon signals, data latency may matter. When the forecast information is delayed by 6 h, the deployable saving on PM100 is \pendingkey{} of run-now emissions, to be read beside the frozen 1.90\,\%.
paper/sections/06_discussion.tex:33:\medskip\noindent\textbf{A. Counterfactual replay on foreign traces.} Marconi100 (2020), Fugaku and KIT ForHLR II (2016 to 2018) are placed on U.S. production signals of the test year, and a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel could not be obtained. The agreement of three foreign traces does not establish U.S. behavior, and a balancing authority's standard time set against trace daylight saving can shift summer arrivals by an hour. The study is therefore a what-if replay, not a deployment forecast. A placement-shift check (exploratory, PM100) moves arrivals by 3 h in either direction; the saving of D changes by \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} percentage points for the earlier and the later shift (Section~\ref{sec:results}).
paper/sections/06_discussion.tex:35:\medskip\noindent\textbf{B. Average, not marginal, intensity.} The signal is production-average intensity from EIA-930; imports and storage are excluded and three of the nine emission factors are unverified. A saving in accounted intensity need not equal avoided marginal emissions, and the ordering of arms may change under a marginal signal. The claim is limited to modeled operational emissions on average intensity. S3 refits the factor sets (\pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} for D on PM100), and X7 uses a fossil-only proxy.
paper/sections/06_discussion.tex:39:\medskip\noindent\textbf{D. No queue or capacity.} The replay has no queue, backfilling, idle power or interruptibility, and delayed jobs may concentrate load. The savings are bounds under free capacity, and interruptible jobs are not covered. X4 is a congestion indicator only: the hourly peak-to-mean ratio of D relative to run-now is \pendingkey{} on PM100, and it cannot establish feasibility.
paper/sections/06_discussion.tex:41:\medskip\noindent\textbf{E. Interval scope.} The calendar interval is conditional on the observed job sample and treats BAs as independent; PM100 is one 2020 trace of 394 users after filtering. The synchronized-week interval (PH1) changes the width of the D interval by factors of 1.71, 0.93 and 2.28, while both intervals condition on the sampled jobs. The exploratory workload-sample check (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty and gives \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{} for D on PM100.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:15:In relative terms, $D$ saves 1.90\,\% of run-now emissions on PM100 (calendar interval 1.81\,\% to 1.99\,\%; synchronized-week interval 1.73\,\% to 2.05\,\%), and H1 is supported ($p<0.0005$, Holm-adjusted $<0.0015$). The perfect-information arm saves 5.75\,\% (calendar interval 5.64\,\% to 5.87\,\%), so $D$ keeps 33.0\,\% of it (calendar interval 31.7\,\% to 34.2\,\%; synchronized-week interval 31.1\,\% to 34.6\,\%). This retained share is pooled, carbon-weighted and conditional on this workload and slack, and it is not a population range. A start shift of at most 12 h can reach only hours within half a day of arrival. The planner sees a past-only forecast of those hours and a requested walltime that is long for most jobs (among the analyzed jobs the median ratio of actual to requested runtime is 0.14). Both would limit what the replay can recover. Figure~\ref{fig:savings} shows all arms on the three traces.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:17:The baseline ladder is in Table~\ref{tab:confirm}, Panel D. On PM100 the night rule saves 0.29\,\%, and persistence with the requested walltime saves $-$0.20\,\%, a loss. Seasonal naive day and week save 0.96\,\% and 1.16\,\%, profile7 1.11\,\% and the historical profile 0.73\,\%, all with the requested walltime. On every trace and at every slack, $D$ saves more than the night rule and more than each of these past-only signals paired with the requested walltime. Among the signals tested, the trained forecaster therefore gives the highest saving under the requested walltime.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:21:\emph{Secondary (forecast skill), post-hoc and descriptive (decomposition), confirmatory on PM100 (H3).} Forecast skill is read before the split because it sets the forecast term. On the test year, the median over BAs of the mean absolute error at leads of 1 to 24 h is 22.0 g/kWh for LightGBM, against 24.0 for seasonal naive day, 27.8 for profile7 and 42.3 for persistence. LightGBM has a lower error than seasonal naive day in 37 of 42 BAs. At leads of 49 to 96 h the medians are 30.6 and 35.4 g/kWh for LightGBM and seasonal naive day (Figure~\ref{fig:forecast}). Error alone does not say whether the forecaster matters for the saving. Replacing it by profile7 under the requested walltime lowers the saving by 0.79\,pp on PM100 (calendar interval 0.71\,pp to 0.87\,pp; synchronized-week interval 0.66\,pp to 0.90\,pp), by 0.47\,pp on F-DATA and by 0.53\,pp on KIT FH2. All three intervals exclude zero under both bootstraps, which suggests that the trained forecaster adds saving beyond a profile of recent hours.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:25:History prediction recovers part of the runtime term. The prediction recovery H3 is 1.03\,pp on PM100 (calendar interval 0.96\,pp to 1.10\,pp; synchronized-week interval 0.93\,pp to 1.13\,pp), and H3 is supported ($p<0.0005$, Holm-adjusted $<0.0015$). The arm \Arm{lgbm}{pred} saves 2.93\,\% and keeps 50.9\,\% of the perfect-information saving, while \Arm{lgbm}{pred2} saves 2.37\,\%. \Arm{lgbm}{pred} is the best registered arm that uses neither the actual runtime nor the realized intensity on every trace, so its margin over $D$ equals H3 (1.03\,pp on PM100). A prediction from the user's past jobs narrows the distance between the believed and the actual runtime, which is the likely route to the recovery; the replay does not test the route directly.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:41:\emph{Secondary; replications in direction, never confirmations.} The three contrasts have the sign found on PM100 on both other traces, and their calendar intervals exclude zero (Table~\ref{tab:confirm}, Panel B). On F-DATA, $D$ saves 3.81\,\%, the runtime penalty is 1.50\,pp and the prediction recovery 0.53\,pp. On KIT FH2 the values are 1.51\,\%, 1.31\,pp and 0.35\,pp. The registered tests cover PM100 only, so these are replications of direction and size and carry no p-value. The retained share is 52.8\,\% on F-DATA (calendar interval 49.7\,\% to 55.5\,\%) and 37.6\,\% on KIT FH2 (36.9\,\% to 38.2\,\%), against 33.0\,\% on PM100 (Table~\ref{tab:secondary}). The runtime term exceeds the forecast term on KIT FH2 as on PM100, and not on F-DATA (Section~\ref{sec:results:decomp}). F-DATA has the tightest requests, with a median actual-to-requested ratio of 0.31 against 0.14 on PM100 and 0.18 on KIT FH2. With three traces, that ordering is an observation and not a test of why the runtime term differs. KIT FH2 carries an assumed 1 kW for every job, so its energy weights follow runtime only (Section~\ref{sec:data:jobs}).
paper/sections/05_results.tex:48:\emph{Frozen variants are secondary; the synchronized-week interval is post-hoc; S1 to S3, X1 and X4 are exploratory and post-freeze, and S4 is secondary, run after the freeze (PREREG item 6; deviation row 2). Cells that depend on queued runs are marked pending in Table~\ref{tab:robust}.} Synchronizing the weeks widens the interval of $D$ by a factor of 1.71 on PM100 and 2.28 on KIT FH2, and leaves it about as wide on F-DATA (0.93). The intervals of H1 to H3 still exclude zero on every trace. The two-stage bootstrap, which resamples BAs and then blocks, gives an interval of 1.45\,\% to 2.39\,\% for $D$ on PM100, which also excludes zero. The calendar and synchronized-week intervals describe weather and calendar variation over the fixed BAs, the two-stage interval adds variation across BAs, and none of them describes the job sample. The exploratory resampling of users (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty: the standard deviation of $D$ across the outer samples is \pendingkey{}, against \pendingkey{} in the original-users control, the total-variance interval is \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{}, and the lowest of the outer samples is \pendingkey{} (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}).
paper/sections/05_results.tex:52:Data latency (S2) tests how far the planner's information can age. With the intensity delayed by 1, 3 and 6 h, $D$ moves from 1.90\,\% to \pendingkey{}, \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{}. Seasonal naive day with the requested walltime moves from 0.96\,\% to \pendingkey{}, \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{}. At 24 and 48 h (S2b) $D$ is \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{}, which spans the publication delay described in Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}. The series holds revised values, so S2 isolates delay and not revision. Refitting the carbon intensity with direct combustion factors (S3a) gives \pendingkey{} for $D$, and a higher oil factor (S3b) gives \pendingkey{}. Including jobs that hit their limit (S4) gives 2.04\,\% on PM100.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:54:\emph{X1, exploratory.} The runtime-estimate sweep scales the actual runtime by a factor $m$ (Figure~\ref{fig:runtimex}). For the LightGBM signal, the saving is \pendingkey{} at $m=\tfrac14$, 4.08\,\% at $m=1$ and \pendingkey{} at $m=8$. The requested walltime has a root-mean-square log error of \pendingkey{} against \pendingkey{} for the history prediction. Two simple operator rules sit between them: a padded estimate (error \pendingkey{}) saves \pendingkey{} and a quantile estimate (error \pendingkey{}) saves \pendingkey{}, against 1.90\,\% for the request and 2.93\,\% for the prediction.
paper/sections/05_results.tex:56:\emph{X4, exploratory.} Shifting starts toward cleaner hours can concentrate load. On PM100, the peak-to-mean ratio of hourly job power under $D$ is \pendingkey{} times that under run-now, and the 99th percentile of hourly power is \pendingkey{} times; for \Arm{oracle}{act} the factors are \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen}). This proxy is computed from start times with no queue, so it cannot show whether a shifted load fits the capacity of a system.
paper/sections/03_data.tex:20:We replay three public traces of production supercomputers (Table~\ref{tab:data}). PM100 comes from the Marconi100 system at CINECA in Bologna and holds Slurm records with sampled node power \citep{antici2023pm100}. It is the primary trace and carries the confirmatory tests. F-DATA comes from the Fugaku system and holds job records with measured energy \citep{antici2024fdata}; we use five months (April 2021 and January, April, July and October 2023). The third trace, KIT FH2, is the ForHLR II log of the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology in the Parallel Workloads Archive \citep{feitelson2014pwa}. PM100 and F-DATA carry the CC-BY-4.0 license. The archive states no license for KIT FH2 and asks users to acknowledge the provider, which we do; we do not redistribute the raw log. F-DATA and KIT FH2 are secondary replications.
paper/sections/03_data.tex:26:The standardization dropped no rows. A job table records completed work and not a queue, so it carries no capacity constraint, and the replay is a bound under free capacity. A fourth trace, the NLR Kestrel jobs data, would have matched both the EIA-930 years and a U.S. site, but its host returned a maintenance error on two attempts and its license could not be read, so it is not used. As a consequence, no trace in the study comes from a U.S. site or from the grid years that are replayed.
paper/sections/01_intro.tex:7:Computing draws a notable share of electricity. Data-center energy use grew far more slowly than workloads up to 2018 \cite{masanet2020}, but high-performance computing (HPC) systems, which serve queues of batch jobs on thousands of nodes, add a carbon footprint that recent estimates describe as significant \cite{li2023sc}. Operational emissions per kilowatt-hour are not constant. The carbon intensity of grid electricity changes by the hour with the generation mix, so the same job emits different amounts of CO$_2$ depending on when and where it runs \cite{dodge2022}. A batch scheduler can use this variation without new hardware, because many jobs tolerate a late start and the scheduler may hold a job, within a window of slack, until a lower-carbon hour. Doing so requires two inputs that a deployed scheduler must supply for itself: a forecast of carbon intensity and an estimate of how long each job will run.
paper/sections/01_intro.tex:11:A deployed HPC scheduler faces a different input set. Its forecast is built from past data only, and its runtime information is the walltime that the user requested at submission, an upper bound that the system enforces by killing the job. Users tend to request far more than they need. Average runtime-to-estimate accuracy was between 31 and 47 percent on four production logs \cite{tsafrir2007}, and on the three traces used here the median analyzed job ran for 14.3\,\%, 30.6\,\% and 17.8\,\% of its requested time (PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2).
paper/sections/02_related.tex:21:A requested walltime is an upper bound that users set above the expected runtime. Mu'alem and Feitelson \cite{mualem2001} find user estimates on three IBM SP2 logs to be rather poor but a good upper bound, because few jobs are killed at their limit. They note that inaccurate estimates need not harm a backfilling scheduler, so the requested-walltime arm here is a loss of planning information, not a defect of the scheduler. Tsafrir et al.\ \cite{tsafrir2007} keep user estimates only as kill times and schedule with the mean runtime of the user's last two jobs, raising average accuracy from between 31 and 47 percent to about 60 to 62 percent on four logs. Machine-learning predictors \cite{gaussier2015}, the penalty of underestimates that kill jobs \cite{fan2017}, and a trace with refined runtime estimates \cite{klusacek2024} extend this line; a status report on parallel job scheduling \cite{feitelson2004} gives the background. These works target waiting time and utilization, not carbon.

