paper/sections\05_results.tex:48:\emph{Frozen variants are secondary; the synchronized-week interval is post-hoc; S1 to S3, X1 and X4 are exploratory and post-freeze, and S4 is secondary, run after the freeze (PREREG item 6; deviation row 2). Cells that depend on queued runs are marked pending in Table~\ref{tab:robust}.} Synchronizing the weeks widens the interval of $D$ by a factor of 1.71 on PM100 and 2.28 on KIT FH2, and leaves it about as wide on F-DATA (0.93). The intervals of H1 to H3 still exclude zero on every trace. The two-stage bootstrap, which resamples BAs and then blocks, gives an interval of 1.45\,\% to 2.39\,\% for $D$ on PM100, which also excludes zero. The calendar and synchronized-week intervals describe weather and calendar variation over the fixed BAs, the two-stage interval adds variation across BAs, and none of them describes the job sample. The exploratory resampling of users (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty: the standard deviation of $D$ across the outer samples is \pendingkey{}, against \pendingkey{} in the original-users control, the total-variance interval is \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{}, and the lowest of the outer samples is \pendingkey{} (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}).
paper/sections\05_results.tex:52:Data latency (S2) tests how far the planner's information can age. With the intensity delayed by 1, 3 and 6 h, $D$ moves from 1.90\,\% to \pendingkey{}, \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{}. Seasonal naive day with the requested walltime moves from 0.96\,\% to \pendingkey{}, \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{}. At 24 and 48 h (S2b) $D$ is \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{}, which spans the publication delay described in Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}. The series holds revised values, so S2 isolates delay and not revision. Refitting the carbon intensity with direct combustion factors (S3a) gives \pendingkey{} for $D$, and a higher oil factor (S3b) gives \pendingkey{}. Including jobs that hit their limit (S4) gives 2.04\,\% on PM100.
paper/sections\05_results.tex:54:\emph{X1, exploratory.} The runtime-estimate sweep scales the actual runtime by a factor $m$ (Figure~\ref{fig:runtimex}). For the LightGBM signal, the saving is \pendingkey{} at $m=\tfrac14$, 4.08\,\% at $m=1$ and \pendingkey{} at $m=8$. The requested walltime has a root-mean-square log error of \pendingkey{} against \pendingkey{} for the history prediction. Two simple operator rules sit between them: a padded estimate (error \pendingkey{}) saves \pendingkey{} and a quantile estimate (error \pendingkey{}) saves \pendingkey{}, against 1.90\,\% for the request and 2.93\,\% for the prediction.
paper/sections\05_results.tex:56:\emph{X4, exploratory.} Shifting starts toward cleaner hours can concentrate load. On PM100, the peak-to-mean ratio of hourly job power under $D$ is \pendingkey{} times that under run-now, and the 99th percentile of hourly power is \pendingkey{} times; for \Arm{oracle}{act} the factors are \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen}). This proxy is computed from start times with no queue, so it cannot show whether a shifted load fits the capacity of a system.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tex:2:% Numbers enter only as <<key|fmt>>; <<PENDING:key|fmt>> marks keys that queued sensitivity runs will write (names from src/sens_*.py).
paper/sections\06_discussion.tex:21:For providers of carbon signals, data latency may matter. When the forecast information is delayed by 6 h, the deployable saving on PM100 is \pendingkey{} of run-now emissions, to be read beside the frozen 1.90\,\%.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tex:33:\medskip\noindent\textbf{A. Counterfactual replay on foreign traces.} Marconi100 (2020), Fugaku and KIT ForHLR II (2016 to 2018) are placed on U.S. production signals of the test year, and a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel could not be obtained. The agreement of three foreign traces does not establish U.S. behavior, and a balancing authority's standard time set against trace daylight saving can shift summer arrivals by an hour. The study is therefore a what-if replay, not a deployment forecast. A placement-shift check (exploratory, PM100) moves arrivals by 3 h in either direction; the saving of D changes by \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} percentage points for the earlier and the later shift (Section~\ref{sec:results}).
paper/sections\06_discussion.tex:35:\medskip\noindent\textbf{B. Average, not marginal, intensity.} The signal is production-average intensity from EIA-930; imports and storage are excluded and three of the nine emission factors are unverified. A saving in accounted intensity need not equal avoided marginal emissions, and the ordering of arms may change under a marginal signal. The claim is limited to modeled operational emissions on average intensity. S3 refits the factor sets (\pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} for D on PM100), and X7 uses a fossil-only proxy.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tex:39:\medskip\noindent\textbf{D. No queue or capacity.} The replay has no queue, backfilling, idle power or interruptibility, and delayed jobs may concentrate load. The savings are bounds under free capacity, and interruptible jobs are not covered. X4 is a congestion indicator only: the hourly peak-to-mean ratio of D relative to run-now is \pendingkey{} on PM100, and it cannot establish feasibility.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tex:41:\medskip\noindent\textbf{E. Interval scope.} The calendar interval is conditional on the observed job sample and treats BAs as independent; PM100 is one 2020 trace of 394 users after filtering. The synchronized-week interval (PH1) changes the width of the D interval by factors of 1.71, 0.93 and 2.28, while both intervals condition on the sampled jobs. The exploratory workload-sample check (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty and gives \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{} for D on PM100.
paper/sections\05_results.tmpl.tex:48:\emph{Frozen variants are secondary; the synchronized-week interval is post-hoc; S1 to S3, X1 and X4 are exploratory and post-freeze, and S4 is secondary, run after the freeze (PREREG item 6; deviation row 2). Cells that depend on queued runs are marked pending in Table~\ref{tab:robust}.} Synchronizing the weeks widens the interval of $D$ by a factor of <<posthoc.PH1.PM100.w.S12.D.widen|.2f>> on PM100 and <<posthoc.PH1.KIT_FH2.w.S12.D.widen|.2f>> on KIT FH2, and leaves it about as wide on F-DATA (<<posthoc.PH1.FDATA.w.S12.D.widen|.2f>>). The intervals of H1 to H3 still exclude zero on every trace. The two-stage bootstrap, which resamples BAs and then blocks, gives an interval of <<TEST.PM100.w.twostage.S12.H1_deployable_saving.lo|pct2>> to <<TEST.PM100.w.twostage.S12.H1_deployable_saving.hi|pct2>> for $D$ on PM100, which also excludes zero. The calendar and synchronized-week intervals describe weather and calendar variation over the fixed BAs, the two-stage interval adds variation across BAs, and none of them describes the job sample. The exploratory resampling of users (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty: the standard deviation of $D$ across the outer samples is <<PENDING:sens.s1.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.outer_sd|pp2>>, against <<PENDING:sens.s1ctl.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.outer_sd|pp2>> in the original-users control, the total-variance interval is <<PENDING:sens.s1.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.tot_lo|pct2>> to <<PENDING:sens.s1.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.tot_hi|pct2>>, and the lowest of the outer samples is <<PENDING:sens.s1.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.outer_min|pct2>> (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}).
paper/sections\05_results.tmpl.tex:52:Data latency (S2) tests how far the planner's information can age. With the intensity delayed by 1, 3 and 6 h, $D$ moves from <<TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> to <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k1.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>>, <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k3.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> and <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k6.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>>. Seasonal naive day with the requested walltime moves from <<TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.snaive_day|req|pct2>> to <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k1.saving.snaive_day|req|pct2>>, <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k3.saving.snaive_day|req|pct2>> and <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k6.saving.snaive_day|req|pct2>>. At 24 and 48 h (S2b) $D$ is <<PENDING:sens.s2b.PM100.w.S12.k24.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> and <<PENDING:sens.s2b.PM100.w.S12.k48.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>>, which spans the publication delay described in Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}. The series holds revised values, so S2 isolates delay and not revision. Refitting the carbon intensity with direct combustion factors (S3a) gives <<PENDING:sens.s3a.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est|pct2>> for $D$, and a higher oil factor (S3b) gives <<PENDING:sens.s3b.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est|pct2>>. Including jobs that hit their limit (S4) gives <<sens.s4.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est|pct2>> on PM100.
paper/sections\05_results.tmpl.tex:54:\emph{X1, exploratory.} The runtime-estimate sweep scales the actual runtime by a factor $m$ (Figure~\ref{fig:runtimex}). For the LightGBM signal, the saving is <<PENDING:sens.x1.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|m0.25|pct2>> at $m=\tfrac14$, <<TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|act|pct2>> at $m=1$ and <<PENDING:sens.x1.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|m8|pct2>> at $m=8$. The requested walltime has a root-mean-square log error of <<PENDING:sens.arms.PM100.rt_req.rms_log|.2f>> against <<PENDING:sens.arms.PM100.rt_pred.rms_log|.2f>> for the history prediction. Two simple operator rules sit between them: a padded estimate (error <<PENDING:sens.arms.PM100.rt_pad.rms_log|.2f>>) saves <<PENDING:sens.arms.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|pad|pct2>> and a quantile estimate (error <<PENDING:sens.arms.PM100.rt_q.rms_log|.2f>>) saves <<PENDING:sens.arms.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|q|pct2>>, against <<TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> for the request and <<TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|pred|pct2>> for the prediction.
paper/sections\05_results.tmpl.tex:56:\emph{X4, exploratory.} Shifting starts toward cleaner hours can concentrate load. On PM100, the peak-to-mean ratio of hourly job power under $D$ is <<PENDING:sens.x4.PM100.lgbm|req.ptm_rel.mean|.2f>> times that under run-now, and the 99th percentile of hourly power is <<PENDING:sens.x4.PM100.lgbm|req.p99_rel.mean|.2f>> times; for \Arm{oracle}{act} the factors are <<PENDING:sens.x4.PM100.oracle|act.ptm_rel.mean|.2f>> and <<PENDING:sens.x4.PM100.oracle|act.p99_rel.mean|.2f>> (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen}). This proxy is computed from start times with no queue, so it cannot show whether a shifted load fits the capacity of a system.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tmpl.tex:2:% Numbers enter only as <<key|fmt>>; <<PENDING:key|fmt>> marks keys that queued sensitivity runs will write (names from src/sens_*.py).
paper/sections\06_discussion.tmpl.tex:21:For providers of carbon signals, data latency may matter. When the forecast information is delayed by 6 h, the deployable saving on PM100 is <<PENDING:sens.s2.PM100.w.S12.k6.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> of run-now emissions, to be read beside the frozen <<TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est|pct2>>.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tmpl.tex:33:\medskip\noindent\textbf{A. Counterfactual replay on foreign traces.} Marconi100 (2020), Fugaku and KIT ForHLR II (2016 to 2018) are placed on U.S. production signals of the test year, and a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel could not be obtained. The agreement of three foreign traces does not establish U.S. behavior, and a balancing authority's standard time set against trace daylight saving can shift summer arrivals by an hour. The study is therefore a what-if replay, not a deployment forecast. A placement-shift check (exploratory, PM100) moves arrivals by 3 h in either direction; the saving of D changes by <<PENDING:sens.shift.PM100.w.S12.diff_vs_z0.m3.lgbm|req|pct2>> and <<PENDING:sens.shift.PM100.w.S12.diff_vs_z0.p3.lgbm|req|pct2>> percentage points for the earlier and the later shift (Section~\ref{sec:results}).
paper/sections\06_discussion.tmpl.tex:35:\medskip\noindent\textbf{B. Average, not marginal, intensity.} The signal is production-average intensity from EIA-930; imports and storage are excluded and three of the nine emission factors are unverified. A saving in accounted intensity need not equal avoided marginal emissions, and the ordering of arms may change under a marginal signal. The claim is limited to modeled operational emissions on average intensity. S3 refits the factor sets (<<PENDING:sens.s3a.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> and <<PENDING:sens.s3b.PM100.w.S12.saving.lgbm|req|pct2>> for D on PM100), and X7 uses a fossil-only proxy.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tmpl.tex:39:\medskip\noindent\textbf{D. No queue or capacity.} The replay has no queue, backfilling, idle power or interruptibility, and delayed jobs may concentrate load. The savings are bounds under free capacity, and interruptible jobs are not covered. X4 is a congestion indicator only: the hourly peak-to-mean ratio of D relative to run-now is <<PENDING:sens.x4.PM100.lgbm|req.ptm_rel.mean|.2f>> on PM100, and it cannot establish feasibility.
paper/sections\06_discussion.tmpl.tex:41:\medskip\noindent\textbf{E. Interval scope.} The calendar interval is conditional on the observed job sample and treats BAs as independent; PM100 is one 2020 trace of <<data.PM100.users_filtered|int>> users after filtering. The synchronized-week interval (PH1) changes the width of the D interval by factors of <<posthoc.PH1.PM100.w.S12.D.widen|.2f>>, <<posthoc.PH1.FDATA.w.S12.D.widen|.2f>> and <<posthoc.PH1.KIT_FH2.w.S12.D.widen|.2f>>, while both intervals condition on the sampled jobs. The exploratory workload-sample check (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty and gives <<PENDING:sens.s1.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.tot_lo|pct2>> to <<PENDING:sens.s1.PM100.w.S12.D_saving_lgbm_req.tot_hi|pct2>> for D on PM100.
